# Basic Definitions and Results

In this section we collect basic definitions and results about $`C^*`-algebras and
$`W^*`-algebras.

An associative algebra $`\mathcal{A}` over the complex numbers is called a *normed algebra*
if there is a map assigning to every $`x \in \mathcal{A}` a real number $`\|x\|`, and this
map satisfies:

1. $`\|x\| \ge 0` and $`\|x\| = 0 \iff x = 0`;
2. $`\|x + y\| \le \|x\| + \|y\|`;
3. $`\|\lambda x\| = |\lambda| \|x\|`;
4. $`\|xy\| \le \|x\| \|y\|`.

If $`\mathcal{A}` is also complete with respect to the norm, it is called a *Banach algebra*.
A mapping $`{}^* : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A}` is called an *involution* if it satisfies:

1. $`(x^*)^* = x`;
2. $`(x + y)^* = x^* + y^*`;
3. $`(xy)^* = y^* x^*`;
4. $`(\lambda x)^* = \overline{\lambda} x^*`,

where $`\lambda` above is a complex number.

:::definition "def:cstar_def" (tags := "mathlib") (lean := "CStarAlgebra, NonUnitalCStarAlgebra")
A Banach algebra $`\mathcal{A}` with an involution is a $`C^*`-*algebra* if the relation
$`\|x^* x\| = \|x\|^2` holds for every $`x \in \mathcal{A}`. A $`C^*`-algebra is *unital* if
it has a multiplicative identity.
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:star_pres_norm_Sak_1_1_6" (tags := "mathlib") (lean := "CStarRing.to_normedStarGroup") (uses := "def:cstar_def")
(Sakai 1.1.6) The involution on a $`C^*`-algebra preserves the norm.
:::

:::proof "lem:star_pres_norm_Sak_1_1_6"
Note $`\|x\|^2 = \|x^* x\| \le \|x^*\| \|x\|`, and
$`\|x^*\|^2 \le \|x x^*\| \le \|x\| \|x^*\|`.
:::

:::theorem "thm:comm_cstar_cts_fcns_Sak_1_2_1" (tags := "mathlib") (lean := "gelfandStarTransform") (uses := "def:cstar_def")
(Sakai 1.2.1) Every commutative, unital $`C^*`-algebra $`\mathcal{A}` is
$`*`-isomorphic to $`C(K)`, the algebra of complex-valued continuous functions on $`K`,
where $`K` is the compact Hausdorff space of maximal ideals of $`\mathcal{A}`. (Note $`C(K)`
is itself a $`C^*`-algebra with pointwise complex conjugation as $`*` and the uniform norm.)
:::

:::proof "thm:comm_cstar_cts_fcns_Sak_1_2_1"
By Banach algebra theory, the Gelfand transform provides a homomorphism of $`\mathcal{A}`
into $`C(K)`. A standard calculation using the $`C^*`-identity shows that it is isometric.
Mapping a self-adjoint element $`h` to the unit circle (complex exponentiation) one can show
that the spectrum lands on the unit circle and so the spectrum of $`h` is in the reals. The
spectrum of an element in $`C(K)` is its range, from which $`*`-preservation follows.
:::

:::corollary "cor:nonunital_gelfand_Sak_1_2_2" (uses := "thm:comm_cstar_cts_fcns_Sak_1_2_1")
(Sakai 1.2.2) Let $`\mathcal{A}` be a commutative $`C^*`-algebra without identity. Then
$`\mathcal{A}` is $`*`-isomorphic to the algebra $`C_0(\Omega)` of continuous functions
vanishing at infinity on a locally compact Hausdorff space $`\Omega`. The space $`\Omega` is
called the *spectrum space* of $`\mathcal{A}`.
:::

:::corollary "cor:spec_sa_homeo_spec_cstar" (tags := "mathlib") (lean := "cfcₙ, IsSelfAdjoint.instNonUnitalIsometricContinuousFunctionalCalculus") (uses := "thm:comm_cstar_cts_fcns_Sak_1_2_1, lem:homeo_of_cts_bij_cpt_haus")
(Non-unital continuous functional calculus) Let $`a` be a self-adjoint element of a
$`C^*`-algebra $`\mathcal{A}`. Then the $`C^*`-subalgebra of $`\mathcal{A}` generated by $`a` is
isometrically $`*`-isomorphic to $`C_0(\sigma(a) \cup \{0\})`, the algebra of complex-valued
continuous functions on $`\sigma(a) \cup \{0\}` that vanish at $`0`, via an isomorphism sending
$`a` to the identity function.
:::

:::proof "cor:spec_sa_homeo_spec_cstar"
Pass to the unitization $`\tilde{\mathcal{A}}` of $`\mathcal{A}`, in which the spectrum of $`a`
is $`\sigma(a) \cup \{0\}`. The unital $`C^*`-subalgebra of $`\tilde{\mathcal{A}}` generated by
$`a` is commutative, so by {bpref "thm:comm_cstar_cts_fcns_Sak_1_2_1"}[] it is $`C(K)` for its
maximal ideal space $`K`. The Gelfand transform of $`a` is a continuous map
$`K \to \sigma(a) \cup \{0\}`, which is bijective since $`a` generates the algebra, and hence a
homeomorphism by {bpref "lem:homeo_of_cts_bij_cpt_haus"}[]. This identifies the unital
$`C^*`-algebra generated by $`a` with $`C(\sigma(a) \cup \{0\})`, sending $`a` to the identity
function, and the (non-unital) $`C^*`-algebra generated by $`a` corresponds to the functions
vanishing at $`0`.
:::

:::definition "def:wstar_def" (lean := "Predual") (uses := "def:cstar_def")
A $`C^*`-algebra $`M` is called a $`W^*`-*algebra* if it is a dual Banach space, i.e. if
there exists a Banach space $`M_*` so that $`(M_*)^*` is isometrically isomorphic to $`M`.
We call $`M_*` a *predual* of $`M`.
:::

It is premature to fix a notation like $`M_*` in the above, but the aim of this project is to
prove that any two preduals of $`M` are isomorphic as Banach spaces, so we take the liberty
with Sakai of fixing a reference predual at the outset. The formalization also takes $`M` to be
unital from the start; this loses no generality, by {bpref "lem:wstar_unital"}[].

:::lemma_ "lem:sigma_closed_star_subalg_wstar" (lean := "Ultraweak.predualOfIsClosed, Submodule.equivDualQuotientPolarSubmodule") (uses := "def:wstar_def, def:sigma_top, cor:bipolar_submodule")
(Sakai 1.1.4) A self-adjoint, $`\sigma`-closed $`*`-subalgebra $`N` of a $`W^*`-algebra $`M`
is also a $`W^*`-algebra, since $`(M_* / N^0)^* = N`, where $`N^0` is the polar
(annihilator) of $`N` in $`M_*`, i.e. the set of $`\varphi \in M_*` such that
$`\varphi(x) = 0` for all $`x \in N`.
:::

:::proof "lem:sigma_closed_star_subalg_wstar"
Restriction to $`N` identifies the dual of $`M_* / N^0` isometrically with the subspace of
$`M = (M_*)^*` annihilating $`N^0`, and by {bpref "cor:bipolar_submodule"}[] this is the
$`\sigma`-closure of $`N`, which is $`N` itself.
:::

# The Ultraweak (Sigma) Topology

:::definition "def:sigma_top" (lean := "Ultraweak") (uses := "def:wstar_def, def:weak_top")
The weak-$`*` topology $`\sigma(M, M_*)` on a $`W^*`-algebra $`M`, that is, the weak topology of
the pairing $`M \times M_* \to \mathbb{C}` ({bpref "def:weak_top"}[]), is called the
$`\sigma`-*topology*, or *ultraweak topology*.
:::

:::proposition "prop:loc_cvx_result" (lean := "Ultraweak.continuous_iff_exists_eq, Ultraweak.continuous_iff_mem_range") (uses := "def:sigma_top, lem:weak_dual, def:compatible_dual")
The set of $`\sigma(M, M_*)`-continuous linear functionals is precisely $`M_*`.
:::

:::proof "prop:loc_cvx_result"
This is {bpref "lem:weak_dual"}[] for the pairing of $`M` with $`M_*`, which separates the points
of $`M_*`. Equivalently, $`\sigma(M, M_*)` is compatible with this pairing
({bpref "def:compatible_dual"}[]).
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:cut_down_sigma_closed_cts" (lean := "IsStarProjection.isClosed_cornerSubmodule_of_ultraweak") (uses := "def:sigma_top, thm:krein_smulian, lem:pos_sa_sigma_closed_Sak_1_7_1")
(Sakai 1.7.6) Let $`p` be any projection in $`M`. Then the subspace $`pMp` is $`\sigma`-closed.
:::

:::proof "lem:cut_down_sigma_closed_cts"
By {bpref "thm:krein_smulian"}[] it suffices to show that $`pMp \cap S` is $`\sigma`-closed, where
$`S` is the closed unit ball, and since $`x \mapsto x^*` is $`\sigma`-continuous
({bpref "lem:pos_sa_sigma_closed_Sak_1_7_1"}[]) it suffices to treat the self-adjoint part. If
$`P` denotes the positive elements, then
$`pM^sp \cap S = (pPp \cap S) - (pPp \cap S) = ([0, p] \cap S) - ([0, p] \cap S)`. The order
interval $`[0, p]` is $`\sigma`-closed by {bpref "lem:pos_sa_sigma_closed_Sak_1_7_1"}[], and $`S`
is $`\sigma`-compact, so this is the continuous image of a compact set, hence closed.

That $`x \mapsto pxp` is $`\sigma`-continuous, which Sakai also includes in 1.7.6, follows from
{bpref "lem:star_left_mul_right_mul_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_8"}[].
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:cut_down_of_wstar" (lean := "IsStarProjection.Corner.instCStarAlgebra, IsStarProjection.Corner.instPredual") (uses := "def:sigma_top, lem:cut_down_sigma_closed_cts, lem:sigma_closed_star_subalg_wstar")
If $`p \in M` is a projection, then $`pMp` is also a $`W^*`-algebra with identity $`p`.
:::

:::proof "lem:cut_down_of_wstar"
It is clear that $`eMe` is an algebra, since any sum or product of elements preserves the
pre- and post-multiplication by $`e`. It is also clear that $`e` is the multiplicative
identity of this algebra. The product-reversing property of $`*` and the fact that $`e` is a
self-adjoint projection imply that $`eMe` is a $`*`-algebra. By
{bpref "lem:sigma_closed_star_subalg_wstar"}[], if this $`*`-subalgebra is $`\sigma`-closed,
then it is a $`W^*`-subalgebra of $`M`. But this is the result of
{bpref "lem:cut_down_sigma_closed_cts"}[].
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:left_mul_proj_right_mul_proj_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_7" (lean := "Ultraweak.continuous_mul_left_of_isStarProjection") (uses := "def:sigma_top, lem:cut_down_sigma_closed_cts, thm:krein_smulian")
(Sakai 1.7.7) If $`p` is any projection of $`M`, then the mappings $`x \mapsto px` and
$`x \mapsto xp` are $`\sigma`-continuous.
:::

:::proof "lem:left_mul_proj_right_mul_proj_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_7"
We follow arXiv:2009.05825, §5.3. Let $`q = 1 - p` and let $`S` be the closed unit ball. For
$`y \in S`, $`\lambda \in \mathbb{C}` and $`b \in qMp`, the $`C^*`-identity gives
$`\|pyq + \lambda p\| \le (1 + |\lambda|^2)^{1/2}` and $`\|pyq + b\| \le \max(1, \|b\|)`.
Since closed balls are $`\sigma`-closed, these estimates pass to $`\sigma`-limits $`x` of nets
$`(p y_\alpha q)`, and force $`pxp = qxq = qxp = 0`, so that $`x \in pSq`. Thus $`pSq` is
$`\sigma`-compact. Since also $`pSp` is $`\sigma`-compact ({bpref "lem:cut_down_sigma_closed_cts"}[]),
so is $`pS = (pSp + pSq) \cap S`, and by {bpref "thm:krein_smulian"}[] $`pM` is
$`\sigma`-closed; likewise $`qM`. Hence $`x \mapsto px` is a bounded idempotent map whose range
$`pM` and kernel $`qM` are $`\sigma`-closed, and such a map is $`\sigma`-continuous (by
compactness of the unit ball and {bpref "thm:krein_smulian"}[]). The claim for $`x \mapsto xp`
follows by taking adjoints.
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:star_left_mul_right_mul_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_8" (lean := "Ultraweak.instSeparatelyContinuousMul, Ultraweak.instIsSemitopologicalRing") (uses := "def:sigma_top, lem:left_mul_proj_right_mul_proj_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_7, lem:wstar_pos_approx_proj, prop:loc_cvx_result")
(Sakai 1.7.8) The mappings $`x \mapsto x^*`, $`x \mapsto ax`, and $`x \mapsto xa` are
$`\sigma`-continuous for $`x, a \in M`.
:::

:::proof "lem:star_left_mul_right_mul_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_8"
The set of $`a` for which $`x \mapsto ax` is $`\sigma`-continuous is a linear subspace
containing the projections ({bpref "lem:left_mul_proj_right_mul_proj_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_7"}[]). It is
norm closed, because the $`\sigma`-continuous functionals form a norm closed subspace of $`M^*`
({bpref "prop:loc_cvx_result"}[]). Since the span of the projections is norm dense
({bpref "lem:wstar_pos_approx_proj"}[]), it is all of $`M`. The claim for $`x \mapsto xa` follows
by taking adjoints.
:::
